load @term/seed/algebra/normed-division
  find normed-division-algebra

# The real numbers, the base of the number tower, dimension one. They are the unique complete ordered field: ordered, so
# every real sits definitely left or right of every other, and complete, so there are no gaps and every bounded set has
# a least upper bound. As a normed division algebra they are the first rung, the ground the Cayley-Dickson doubling
# starts from, and the only rung that keeps the order (the very next rung, the complex numbers, carries a square root of
# minus one whose square is below zero, and no order can place it). In the substrate a real number is never a base
# thing but the mood of many ternary grains, the average over a crowd of discrete tones.
form real
  link algebra, like normed-division-algebra
